I see. The sandbox is restrictive; `npm install` would be denied if it requires network or "programs". I need to confirm I can install dependencies and run tests before relying on them.The sandbox seems to block npm install via policy (likely network). Let me try a different approach.Typecheck works with globally available tools. Now let me think about the bug carefully.

Looking at `src/index.ts` line 82-91:

```ts
off<Key extends keyof Events>(type: Key, handler?: GenericEventHandler) {
    const handlers: Array<GenericEventHandler> | undefined = all!.get(type);
    if (handlers) {
        if (handler) {
            handlers.splice(handlers.indexOf(handler), 1);
        } else {
            all!.set(type, []);
        }
    }
},
```

The bug: `handlers.indexOf(handler)` returns `-1` when the handler is not found in the array. `splice(-1, 1)` removes the **last** element of the array (not the first, not nothing). So when you call `off(type, handler)` with a handler that wasn't registered, it incorrectly removes the last handler in the list.

This matches the bug report exactly: "calling off(type, handler) with a handler that was never registered for that type removes a different handler."

